WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.464
The Lowry model is an influential framework for allocating urban activities.

2
00:00:04.464 --> 00:00:07.577
It divides employment into basic and service sectors.

3
00:00:07.577 --> 00:00:11.864
Basic employment is treated as an external driver serving a wider market.

4
00:00:11.864 --> 00:00:15.564
Households locate in relation to employment and available land.

5
00:00:15.564 --> 00:00:18.971
Service employment then locates in relation to households.

6
00:00:18.971 --> 00:00:21.731
The process repeats until changes become small.

7
00:00:21.731 --> 00:00:23.728
Accessibility connects the pieces.

8
00:00:23.728 --> 00:00:30.307
Zones that are easier to reach from jobs may attract more households, subject to capacity and other constraints.

9
00:00:30.307 --> 00:00:36.474
Population creates demand for local services, which creates more employment and another allocation round.

10
00:00:36.474 --> 00:00:39.352
Distance or travel impedance reduces interaction.

11
00:00:39.352 --> 00:00:44.520
The model is useful because its logic is clear, but its simplifications are substantial.

12
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.514
The basic-service distinction can be difficult in a complex economy.

13
00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:56.913
Household types, prices, development feasibility, zoning, remote work, and multiple travel modes may be represented only roughly or not at all.

14
00:00:56.913 --> 00:01:01.259
Calibration can make a base year fit without guaranteeing a good forecast.

15
00:01:01.259 --> 00:01:07.250
The source chapter uses the Lowry model as both a method and a foundation for later integrated models.

16
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:13.241
Its enduring lesson is iterative interaction between employment, population, land, and transportation.

17
00:01:13.241 --> 00:01:14.492
Torrance application.

18
00:01:14.492 --> 00:01:18.364
This separate conceptual example cites the Torrance General Plan.

19
00:01:18.364 --> 00:01:22.117
Imagine dividing Torrance and nearby areas into analysis zones.

20
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:26.823
Regional or export-oriented employment would be identified as an initial input.

21
00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:30.636
Residential capacity would reflect adopted land-use assumptions.

22
00:01:30.636 --> 00:01:35.104
Travel impedance would connect workplaces and possible household locations.

23
00:01:35.104 --> 00:01:39.750
Population would then support an estimated amount of local service employment.

24
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:45.111
The exercise would need regional geography because workers and jobs cross city boundaries.

25
00:01:45.111 --> 00:01:56.013
It would also require several household and employment categories, multimodal access, land prices or feasibility, and explicit capacity constraints to avoid an unrealistic allocation.

26
00:01:56.013 --> 00:02:02.625
Results should be tested against observed population and employment, then varied under alternative assumptions.

27
00:02:02.625 --> 00:02:05.187
A converged allocation is not a prediction.

28
00:02:05.187 --> 00:02:08.821
It is the consequence of the inputs and behavioral structure.

29
00:02:08.821 --> 00:02:13.229
No Lowry model is estimated here, and no forecast is offered for Torrance.

30
00:02:13.229 --> 00:02:19.484
The application is a teaching example showing what local data and limitations would have to be addressed.

31
00:02:19.484 --> 00:02:27.347
This episode adapts chapter six of Transportation Land Use Modeling and Policy by Pan and Sharifi, Mavs Open Press, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.

32
00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:31.041
All third-party diagrams and other excluded items are omitted.

33
00:02:31.041 --> 00:02:35.270
Torrance facts are independently summarized from the City General Plan.

34
00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.819
No endorsement is implied.
